FROM jsonb_to_recordset('[{"id":"birdnet:BN03514","name":"Rock Dove","scientific_name":"Columba livia","description":"The rock dove (Columba livia), also known as the common pigeon or \"rock pigeon\", is a member of the bird family Columbidae (doves and pigeons). In common usage, it is often simply referred to as the \"pigeon\", although the rock dove is the wild form of the bird; the pigeons familiar to most people are the domesticated forms of the wild rock dove.\nWild rock doves are uniformly pale grey with two black bars on each wing, with few differences being seen between males and females; i.e. they are not strongly sexually dimorphic. The domestic pigeon, often, but invalidly, called \"Columba livia domestica\", which includes about 1,000 different breeds, is descended from this species. Escaped domestic pigeons are the origin of feral pigeons around the world. Both forms can vary widely in the colour and pattern of their plumage unlike their wild ancestor, being red, brown, checkered, uniformly coloured, or pied.\nHabitats include various open and semi-open environments where they are able to forage on the ground. Cliffs and rock ledges are used for roosting and breeding in the wild. Originating in Southern Europe, North Africa, and Western Asia, when including their domestic and feral descendants, they are an example of a least-concern species per IUCN among birds, being abundant with an estimated population of 17 to 28 million wild and feral birds in Europe alone and up to 120 million worldwide.","image":"https://birdnet.cornell.edu/taxonomy/api/image/Columba%20livia?size=medium","habitat":null,"diet":null,"rarity":"common","is_starter":true,"source_url":"https://birdnet.cornell.edu/taxonomy/species/Columba%20livia","description_source":"wikipedia","description_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock%20dove","image_attribution":{"author":"Birds of Gilgit-Baltistan","license":"cc-by-sa","cropped":true,"url":"https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/466776975/large.jpg","source":"iNaturalist"}},{"id":"birdnet:BN03520","name":"Common Wood Pigeon","scientific_name":"Columba palumbus","description":"The common wood pigeon (Columba palumbus), also known simply as the wood pigeon, is a large species in the dove and pigeon family (Columbidae), native to the western Palearctic. It belongs to the genus Columba, which includes closely related species such as the rock dove (Columba livia). It has a flexible diet, feeding mainly on plant material, including cereals, and is therefore considered an agricultural pest. Wood pigeons are extensively hunted over much of their range, but this does not appear to have a major impact on their population numbers.","image":"https://birdnet.cornell.edu/taxonomy/api/image/Columba%20palumbus?size=medium","habitat":null,"diet":null,"rarity":"common","is_starter":true,"source_url":"https://birdnet.cornell.edu/taxonomy/species/Columba%20palumbus","description_source":"wikipedia","description_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common%20wood%20pigeon","image_attribution":{"author":"hedera.baltica","license":"cc-by-sa","cropped":true,"url":"https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/108521205/large.jpg","source":"iNaturalist"}},{"id":"birdnet:BN03516","name":"Stock Dove","scientific_name":"Columba oenas","description":"The stock dove (Columba oenas) is a species of bird in the family Columbidae, the doves and pigeons. It is widely distributed in the western Palearctic, north to central Scandinavia and south to northwest Africa. Western and southern populations are resident, while northeastern populations are migratory to avoid the colder winters there. It is the type species of the genus Columba.","image":"https://birdnet.cornell.edu/taxonomy/api/image/Columba%20oenas?size=medium","habitat":null,"diet":null,"rarity":"common","is_starter":true,"source_url":"https://birdnet.cornell.edu/taxonomy/species/Columba%20oenas","description_source":"wikipedia","description_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stock%20dove","image_attribution":{"author":"Paul Lewis","license":"cc-by-nc","cropped":true,"url":"https://inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com/photos/119807269/large.jpg","source":"iNaturalist"}}]'::jsonb) AS species (
